WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.802
- Obviously, so much
of our story is built

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.705
on the hero's journey
of overcoming obstacle.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
And yet, everything in
society today is telling us,

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.343
you know, conflict's not okay.

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:11.944
You know, challenge is not okay.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:13.379
We've gotta be safe.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.148 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've gotta be like prevented
from having challenges.

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:19.118
Whereas actually, it's
challenge that enables

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.921
the human spirit to
grow and to overcome.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:24.724
(dramatic music)

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.764 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:00:30.764 --> 00:00:33.600
is the baroness at
the UK House of Lords

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.604
and the CEO of the Alliance
for Responsible Citizenship,

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:39.105
also known as ARC.

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.808 align:start size:97% position:3%
Baroness Philippa Stroud,
welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:45.178
- Thank you so much, Dave,
really great to join you.

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:46.679
- Well I'm very
happy to have you

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.816
because I will be joining you
guys at the ARC conference

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:53.520
in London in about a week and
a half or two weeks or so.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I thought we could
just chat for a few minutes

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.990 align:start size:88% position:13%
about what the
conference is all about,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of how this
originally came from the,

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:02.128
I think I can say the great
mind of Jordan Peterson,

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.863
what the genesis
of all of this was,

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:05.365
and what the intentions are.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:07.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'd love to talk to you
about British politics,

00:01:07.633 --> 00:01:09.168
but I think we have
to focus on that.

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:12.304 align:start size:81% position:19%
So can you talk
about what ARC is,

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:14.140
how Jordan got to you,

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.142
and where you wanna
go with this thing?

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:17.476
- Yeah, thanks, Dave.

00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:20.713
So ARC is really a
movement of people

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.948
who are basically saying

00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:25.951 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we've had enough
of a declinist,

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:30.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
perma-crisis, poly-crisis
type way of thinking.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:33.159
We actually believe that
there is a better story,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:36.128
a better way forward, and
a more hopeful narrative.

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:38.531
And because we so
believe in story,

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.868
because it shapes who we are
and it shapes our direction

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.336
of travel as societies,

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:44.771
we actually want
to come together

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:48.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
to develop a better
story for our society.

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:52.178
But actually, when I think
back to how it all started,

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:57.283
I had invited Jordan
to come and do a talk

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
at the think tank
that I led in London.

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:02.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we had a fantastic
time with him.

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:06.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
The whole time we were
focused on freedom of speech

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:09.228
and higher education and how
we restore freedom of speech

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:12.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
and academic freedom
back to our universities.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.599
And at the end of it,

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:17.536
he went on his way, did a big
tour around Eastern Europe.

00:02:17.536 --> 00:02:19.973 align:start size:97% position:3%
And halfway through that
tour, he rang me and he said,

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.941
"Philippa, everywhere I go,

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.376
there are people,
fantastic leaders,

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:26.246
but they're so isolated.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.414
And they're doing amazing work.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.450
What would happen if we
brought them all together

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:32.952
to do a conference in London?"

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.420
And I thought, "Great story."

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.955
And at the end of it,
he just said to me,

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.757
"Philippa, do you know this
chap called Paul Marshall?"

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.359 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I said, "Yeah,
great friend of mine."

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.227
So I pinged an email and said,

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Hey, Jordan, meet
Paul. Paul, meet Jordan.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:44.631
You're gonna love each other."

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:47.333
Next couple of days,
Paul rings me and says,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
"You know this conference
that you and Jordan are doing?

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.037
I'm in," and I go,

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:52.405 align:start size:81% position:19%
(Dave laughing)
"What conference?"

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.674
Anyway, it kind of just
snowballed from there.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:58.378
And you know when like
an idea's time has come,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.180
it just has momentum behind it

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.781
and people wanting to get
onboard and make it happen?

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:05.952
That's what ARC has been
like from beginning to end.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.720
- I have to say just on a
personal note, as you know,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
I toured with Jordan
in 2018 and '19

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:12.090
about 120 shows, I
think 20 countries,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.327 align:start position:0%
and what you just described
there of all these great people

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:16.595 align:start size:81% position:19%
coming up to him-
- Yeah.

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:17.830
- After the show,
political leaders,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.198 align:start size:72% position:28%
politician-
- Yeah.

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:21.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Politicians, celebrities,
thinkers, authors.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
Everyone was like, "We've
gotta do something."

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.170 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I'm so thrilled
for this thing.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.604
I really can't wait.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:27.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
But do you find it strange,
you mentioned stories,

00:03:27.539 --> 00:03:30.076
and obviously, Jordan has
been a great explainer

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.877
of why stories are important.

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.980 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can't just
give people policy.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.048 align:start size:81% position:19%
Do you find it
odd that the West,

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.351
which has come up with
so much great, you know,

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:40.686 align:start size:84% position:16%
movie making and
books and literature

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:43.489
and all of the stuff that
our culture is made of,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:46.892
we somehow didn't tell a
story to defend ourselves?

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:51.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
Does that seem like a
strange dichotomy to you?

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:55.034
- Yeah, and it's like our
stories got so fragmented.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:59.772
So one of the questions we're
going to be asking at ARC is:

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.307
What can we draw
from our history?

00:04:02.307 --> 00:04:05.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
What is the
narrative, the story,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.580
that can unite us
as we go forward?

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.616
And we're asking like
what story can be drawn

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:12.719
from our liberal
democratic history

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.520
and what story can be drawn

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.322 align:start size:88% position:13%
from our
Judeo-Christian history.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:19.191 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are there stories that
actually can be foundational

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:22.227
to who we are that
can take us forward?

00:04:22.227 --> 00:04:25.197
And obviously, so much
of our story is built

00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:28.067
on the hero's journey
of overcoming obstacle.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:31.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
And yet, everything in
society today is telling us,

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.705
you know, conflict's not okay.

00:04:32.705 --> 00:04:34.306
You know, challenge is not okay.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:35.741
We've gotta be safe.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:38.510 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've gotta be like prevented
from having challenges.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.480
Whereas actually, it's
challenge that enables

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.416
the human spirit to
grow and to overcome.

00:04:44.416 --> 00:04:46.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
So there are lots of
different themes like this

00:04:46.418 --> 00:04:48.821
that we're going to be
exploring at ARC as well.

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:50.856 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, and the conference
is, it's four days,

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:51.690
if I'm not mistaken?

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:52.958
Is it four full days?

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.661
- Yeah, it starts
on Sunday the 29th

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.496 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then it's going
through the 30th, 31st,

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:58.698
and 1st of November.

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:01.300
So Monday, Tuesday, and
Wednesday in London.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:02.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
So exciting.
- Great, so we're gonna link

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.070
to it down below
because, obviously,

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.572
portions of it at least
are open to the public

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:07.673
and we want people to be there-

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:09.308 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- And all of that.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.077
What other types?

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.479
Is it fair to call
it sort of a counter

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.913
to the World Economic Forum,

00:05:14.913 --> 00:05:16.949
sort of a bottom up way
of looking at the world,

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:20.586 align:start size:97% position:3%
as opposed to top down version
of looking at the world?

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:25.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so it is a gathering
of leaders in London,

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's also really
profoundly built

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
around the principle
of subsidiarity.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:34.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
So what am I responsible
for as an individual?

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:37.003
What is my family
responsible for?

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:39.238 align:start size:84% position:16%
What is my community
responsible for?

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then what is my
nation responsible for?

00:05:41.573 --> 00:05:44.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
So instead of hiding
away somewhere

00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:45.978
and coming up with
these big decisions,

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:48.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
then imposing them on society
and pushing them down,

00:05:48.313 --> 00:05:50.583
it's very much coming
up from the principle

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:53.152
of responsibility
at every level,

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:54.987
including the individual.

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:58.257
And then on the night
of the 1st of November,

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
Jordan's throwing open the
doors at ARC at the O2,

00:06:01.293 --> 00:06:03.796 align:start size:88% position:13%
huge place where anyone
can book a ticket.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:06.198
I mean, it takes
20,000 people, ARC,

00:06:06.198 --> 00:06:08.634
and it's literally open to
anyone who can get a ticket.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.371
They're going very fast if
they haven't already gone.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:15.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, and what are some
of the like categories

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:16.675
that you're gonna
be talking about?

00:06:16.675 --> 00:06:18.277 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I think people
understand sorta broadly

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.545
what you're talking about,

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:21.047
but this is gonna
be about science,

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:22.548
it's gonna be about tech,
media, et cetera, right?

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:25.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like it's gonna be sort
of across all disciplines.

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:26.619
- Yeah.

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.488
So the big question
we're asking is:

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:30.223
What is the better story?

00:06:30.223 --> 00:06:31.757
But within that too, it's like:

00:06:31.757 --> 00:06:34.326
What is the better
story for family

00:06:34.326 --> 00:06:35.761
and the social fabric?

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:37.996
What is the better
story for business?

00:06:37.996 --> 00:06:40.600
What is the better story for
energy and the environment?

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.101 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's asking right
across the board

00:06:43.101 --> 00:06:45.671
all these like, what is
the better story in all

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:48.073
of these different
aspects of life.

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:50.309
There's so much that
we want to platform

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:53.145
that I think it's going to
take years two, three, four,

00:06:53.145 --> 00:06:54.413
to platform it all.

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:57.716
But it's all going to be
framed around that lens

00:06:57.716 --> 00:06:59.117
of the better story.

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:01.153
- And is the plan
then, I assume so,

00:07:01.153 --> 00:07:03.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the plan will be,
okay, we're gonna come up

00:07:03.722 --> 00:07:07.693
with sort of specific stories
that can lead us to policies?

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:08.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then we're gonna find-
- Yeah.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:10.496
- The people that might
be in the House of Lords

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.498
or in the United States
Congress or elsewhere

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we're gonna try to
implement some of this stuff?

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.068
- Absolutely.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.270
And what's really exciting too

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that we've got at least
half the shadow cabinet

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.175
from Australia coming.

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:25.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
Three ex-Australia prime
ministers are coming.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've got two congressional
delegations coming

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:28.881
from the US.

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.083
We've got over 100 different MPs

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:34.020
from different parts
of the world coming.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.655 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we have some people
of real influence,

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:39.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we also have 200
business leaders,

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:44.329
another 200 kind of ideas
and influencers as well.

00:07:44.329 --> 00:07:47.867
So I mean, it's going to be
a very rich, rich audience.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.503 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think there's
anyone in the world

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:52.337
that could've pulled
something like this off

00:07:52.337 --> 00:07:53.672
other than Jordan?

00:07:53.672 --> 00:07:55.641
Like when I first heard
about this, I was like,

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:57.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Oh, this is what Jordan
was supposed to do."

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.146 align:start size:88% position:13%
It just seems so
obvious to me in a way.

00:08:01.446 --> 00:08:03.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I mean, he's a
remarkable, you know this,

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:06.185
he's a remarkable individual.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:08.320
And I love working with him.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.789 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think he's an
extraordinary man.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:13.592 align:start size:97% position:3%
But what's been really
great in the ARC conference

00:08:13.592 --> 00:08:15.795 align:start size:88% position:13%
is the ability to gather
other like-minded,

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:18.030
extraordinary people too.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:19.665
We've got over 90 speakers

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:22.067
who are going to be platformed,

00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:26.872
people wanting to come and be
on the stage and contribute.

00:08:26.872 --> 00:08:28.340
And I think, you know,

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.444 align:start position:0%
this sort of change, Jordan
would be the first person to say

00:08:31.444 --> 00:08:33.712
it can't be through one person.

00:08:33.712 --> 00:08:36.215
It has to be through
a movement of people.

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:40.418
But oh my word, he's been the
most extraordinary catalyst.

00:08:40.418 --> 00:08:43.088
- Does it also feel to you that
the timing is kind of right,

00:08:43.088 --> 00:08:44.256 align:start size:84% position:16%
not just because of-
- Yeah.

00:08:44.256 --> 00:08:45.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
- What's been
happening in the world

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:47.092
for the last couple
years, obviously,

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:49.461
but really, even the events
of the last 10 days or so

00:08:49.461 --> 00:08:52.131
really have the West
feeling very shaken.

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:54.233 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I'm seeing
obviously the protests

00:08:54.233 --> 00:08:55.500
that are happening in London,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.503
but they're happening
here in New York City

00:08:57.503 --> 00:08:59.771
and other places here as well.

00:08:59.771 --> 00:09:00.973
- Yeah.

00:09:00.973 --> 00:09:03.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would really agree
with you, Dave.

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:07.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think one of the questions
that we're asking is:

00:09:07.746 --> 00:09:10.049
Where did we lose
our confidence?

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:13.686 align:start size:88% position:13%
And how did we lose
our values in the West?

00:09:13.686 --> 00:09:16.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
And would these
events be happening

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:20.192
if we had more confidence
in who we are as people,

00:09:20.192 --> 00:09:23.262
more confidence in our values?

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:26.932
And I think we've got, you
know, fantastic historians

00:09:26.932 --> 00:09:30.603 align:start size:97% position:3%
like Niall Ferguson who
are going to be joining us.

00:09:30.603 --> 00:09:32.404
We're hoping that we will have

00:09:32.404 --> 00:09:35.307 align:start size:97% position:3%
some extraordinary
spokespeople even from Israel

00:09:35.307 --> 00:09:38.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
who are able to join us
that aren't announced yet.

